$ gedit prog.c &    // Text editor 
-----------------------   For prog.c program's body
#include <stdio.h>
#ifdef ANG 
void salut(){
printf("Hello \n");
}
#else
void salut (){
printf("Salut \n");
}
#endif
int main (){
salut ();
return 0;
}

------------------------
$ gcc -E prog.c -o prog.i  or $ cpp prog.c -o prog.i// For preprocessing stage 
$ ls // we will have prog.c and prog.i
$ gcc -S prog.i -o prog.s  // TO stop after compilation and result an assembler program
$ ls  // prog.c prog.i prog.s
$ gcc -c prog.s -o prog.o  // The assembly of prog.s results prog.o
$ ls  //prog.c prog.i prog.s prog.o
$ gcc prog.o -o prog  // The last step which is the link edition of prog.o to have the machine binary code of the program needed
$ ls  // prog.c prog.i prog.s prog.o prog

In this program we will have Salut in the screen because ANG is not defined
In order to define the macro ANG we use :
1- #define ANG : In the source file before #ifdef ANG 
2- gcc -DANG prog.c -o prog : On the command line ($ gcc -DANG prog.c -o prog)

After applying one option we should :
./prog  // we will have Hello displayed, because this time the macro ANG is defined 
